  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The query is hydrocodone, a 4,5-epoxymorphinan with a 3-methoxy, N-methyl, and 6-keto; the closest no-external-condition morphinan context, samidorphan, has absolute oral bioavailability of 69% (range 60.3–82.6%), well above the 20% threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C05", "C12", "C26"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Other opioid/morphinan contexts report F above threshold: pentazocine 0.68–0.70 in cirrhosis, pethidine 0.80 in cirrhosis, and morphine 27.7–124% in cirrhosis; none of these transferable direct F records is below 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C13", "C20", "C15", "C25", "C03", "C16", "C19", "C24", "C31"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4/L5 absorption/efflux evidence for related morphinans supports a favorable absorption component: codeine has high Caco-2 and small-intestinal permeability similar to metoprolol, dextromethorphan is almost completely absorbed, morphine is rapidly absorbed from the small intestine, buprenorphine has high permeability, and (-)-SPD shows passive diffusion with efflux ratio 1.0.",
      "card_ids": ["C62", "C64", "C69", "C70", "C67", "C68", "C71", "C65", "C73"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records show measurable CYP-mediated metabolism for related morphinans (dextromethorphan CYP3A4 t1/2 12 min; HLM Clint 28.5–41 µL/min/mg; rat liver microsome Clint 70–83 µL/min/mL; sinomenine high metabolite-formation Clint 161–500 µL/min/mg; dimemorfan mouse Clint 51 µL/min/mg), but these are in vitro and mostly for morphinans lacking the 4,5-epoxy bridge and 6-keto, and do not establish F < 20% for hydrocodone; C85 even labels dextromethorphan as a low-clearance positive control.",
      "card_ids": ["C77", "C78", "C80", "C81", "C82", "C83", "C84", "C87", "C88", "C85"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No transferable affirmative low-F evidence is present; the only low record (rat S-RCP 17%) is a different benzamide scaffold and does not support F < 20% for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C35"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "contradictory",
      "decision_effect": "weakened",
      "card_ids": ["C77", "C78", "C80", "C81", "C82", "C83", "C84", "C87", "C88"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct hydrocodone-specific absolute oral bioavailability record is present in the supplied cards.",
    "L6 hepatic-clearance records are for dextromethorphan, sinomenine, and dimemorfan, not hydrocodone; no direct human liver microsome or in vivo clearance measurement for the query is available.",
    "In vitro intrinsic clearance does not directly quantify oral F; high Clint can be offset by high absorption and does not establish F < 20%.",
    "Most structurally similar opioid F records come from cirrhotic patients, where first-pass extraction may be reduced; healthy-subject F for the query may differ.",
    "No IV-matched oral exposure for hydrocodone is provided, so first-pass metabolism is not directly quantified."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F >= 20%) because the query is hydrocodone, a 4,5-epoxymorphinan; direct F records for closely related morphinans (samidorphan 69%, pentazocine/pethidine/morphine in cirrhosis) are all above threshold, and L4/L5 absorption/efflux evidence is favorable. L6 adds in vitro hepatic-clearance data for related morphinans indicating measurable CYP-mediated metabolism, which modestly weakens confidence but does not provide threshold-relevant evidence for F < 20%; no transferable low-F evidence is present."
}

